== Praising God with the Mouth

X. Nothing can be known with certainty about the nature of religious affections from this, that they strongly incline persons to praise and glorify God with their mouths. This is indeed implied in what has just been observed about abounding and spending much time in the external exercises of religion, and it was also hinted at before. But many seem to look on it as a bright evidence of gracious affection when persons appear greatly inclined to praise and magnify God, to have their mouths full of his praises, and to be calling on others with deep feeling to praise and exalt him. So I thought it deserved a more particular consideration.

No Christian will make it an argument against a person that he seems to have such an inclination. Nor can it reasonably be looked on as evidence for a person, if the things already observed and proved are duly considered: #emph[namely,] that persons without grace may have high affections toward God and Christ; that their affections, being strong, may fill their mouths and incline them to speak much and very earnestly about the things they are affected with; and that there may be counterfeits of all kinds of gracious affection. But it will appear more evidently and directly that this is no certain sign of grace if we consider the instances of it the Scripture gives us in those who were graceless. We often have an account of this in the multitude that was present when Christ preached and worked miracles: “And immediately the man got up, picked up his mat, and walked out in front of them all. As a result, they were all astounded and glorified God, saying, ‘We have never seen anything like this!’” (Mark 2:12). So also in Matt 9:8 and Luke 5:26. Also Matt 15:31: “The crowd was amazed when they saw the mute speaking, the crippled restored, the lame walking, and the blind seeing. And they glorified the God of Israel.” So we are told that on the occasion of Christ’s raising the son of the widow of #emph[Nain,] “A sense of awe swept over all of them, and they glorified God. ‘A great prophet has appeared among us!’ they said. ‘God has visited His people!’” (Luke 7:16). So we read of their glorifying Christ, or speaking exceedingly highly of him: “He taught in their synagogues and was glorified by everyone” (Luke 4:15). And how they praised him with loud voices a little before he was crucified, crying, “Hosanna to the Son of David! … Hosanna in the highest! … Blessed is He who comes in the name of the Lord!” And after Christ’s ascension, when the apostles had healed the lame man, we are told that #emph[all the people were glorifying God for what had happened] (Acts 4:21). When the #emph[Gentiles in Antioch of Pisidia] heard from #emph[Paul] and #emph[Barnabas] that God would reject the #emph[Jews] and take the #emph[Gentiles] to be his people in their place, they were affected with this goodness of God to the #emph[Gentiles, and glorified the word of the Lord.] But not all who did so were true believers, only a certain elect number of them, as is suggested in the account we have of it: “When the Gentiles heard this, they rejoiced and glorified the word of the Lord, and all who were appointed for eternal life believed” (Acts 13:48). So of old the children of Israel sang God’s praise at the Red Sea, but soon forgot his works. And it is foretold of false professing Christians, and real enemies of religion, that they would show an eagerness to glorify God: “You#super[+] who tremble at His word, hear the word of the LORD: ‘Your#super[+] brothers who hate you#super[+] and exclude you#super[+] because of My name have said, “Let the LORD be glorified”’” (Isa 66:5).

It is no certain sign that a person is graciously affected if, in the midst of his hopes and comforts, he is greatly affected with God’s unmerited mercy to him who is so unworthy, and seems greatly to exalt and magnify free grace. Those who still remain with unmortified pride and enmity against God may, when they imagine they have received extraordinary kindness from God, cry out about their unworthiness and magnify God’s undeserved goodness to them. And they may do it from no other conviction of their ill desert, and from no higher principle, than #emph[Saul] had. While he still remained with unsubdued pride and #emph[enmity against David,] he was brought, though a king, to acknowledge his unworthiness and #emph[cry out, “I have played the fool and have committed a grave error!”] And with great affection and wonder he magnified and exalted #emph[David’s] unmerited and unequaled kindness to him (1 Sam 24:16–19; 26:21). And they may do it from no higher principle than the one from which #emph[Nebuchadnezzar] was affected with God’s dispensation, which he saw and experienced, and praised, exalted and honored the King of heaven. And both he and #emph[Darius,] in their high affections, called on all nations to praise God (Dan 3:28–30; 4:1–3, 34–35, 37; 6:25–27).
